{"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/screenstates\/accessibility"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"sections":[],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates"]]},"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/Accessibility","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"schemaVersion":{"minor":3,"patch":0,"major":0},"metadata":{"title":"Accessibility","modules":[{"name":"ScreenStates"}],"role":"article","roleHeading":"Article"},"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Everything the default placeholders — and your own custom ones — get for free: VoiceOver, Reduce Motion, Increase Contrast, Dynamic Type, and stable identifiers for XCUITest."}],"kind":"article","seeAlsoSections":[{"title":"Related Documentation","identifiers":["doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenState","doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithSwiftUI","doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithUIKit"],"anchor":"Related-Documentation"}],"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"content","content":[{"text":"Overview","anchor":"Overview","level":2,"type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"A placeholder is often the ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"only"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"type":"text","text":" thing on screen for a few hundred milliseconds, or the only thing a user with a slow connection sees for much longer than that — so it’s worth it being as accessible as the real content it stands in for. The default "},{"code":"ScreenStateDefault{Empty,Loading,Error}View","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"code":"UIView","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"s handle all of this out of the box; this article rounds up what they do and how a fully custom placeholder can match them."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"text":"VoiceOver announcements","anchor":"VoiceOver-announcements","level":2,"type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Each default placeholder announces its own appearance — ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"AccessibilityNotification.Announcement(_:).post()"},{"type":"text","text":" in SwiftUI, "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UIAccessibility.post(notification:.announcement, argument:)"},{"type":"text","text":" in UIKit — so a screen settling into "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".empty"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".loading"},{"type":"text","text":", or "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".error"},{"type":"text","text":" is heard, not just seen."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenState\/accessibilityAnnouncement"},{"type":"text","text":" exposes that same wording for any case, so a fully custom placeholder can announce transitions consistently instead of inventing its own text:"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"syntax":"swift","code":[".onAppear {","    if let announcement = state.accessibilityAnnouncement {","        AccessibilityNotification.Announcement(announcement).post()","    }","}"],"type":"codeListing"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"It’s ","type":"text"},{"code":"nil","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" for ","type":"text"},{"code":".data","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" — your own content is what VoiceOver should read at that point — and, for ","type":"text"},{"code":".empty","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", it’s the default “Nothing Here” text; announce your own custom title directly instead if your placeholder shows one, so VoiceOver matches what’s on screen. See ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithSwiftUI"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithUIKit"},{"type":"text","text":" for the full examples."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"heading","text":"Reduce Motion","level":2,"anchor":"Reduce-Motion"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The default Loading placeholder’s ring spins via a repeating rotation animation — purely decorative, and exactly the kind of large, continuous motion Reduce Motion exists to suppress. "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ScreenStateDefaultLoadingView"},{"type":"text","text":" reads SwiftUI’s "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"accessibilityReduceMotion"},{"type":"text","text":" environment value; "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ScreenStateDefaultLoadingUIView"},{"type":"text","text":" reads "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UIAccessibility.isReduceMotionEnabled"},{"type":"text","text":" and also observes "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UIAccessibility.reduceMotionStatusDidChangeNotification"},{"type":"text","text":", so an already-visible spinner responds immediately if the setting changes while on screen, not just on the next appearance. Either way, the ring stays static — still colorful, just not spinning — instead of disabling itself entirely, since a static ring still communicates “loading” on its own."}]},{"type":"heading","text":"Increase Contrast","level":2,"anchor":"Increase-Contrast"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"All three default placeholders’ gradient icons\/ring respect Increase Contrast — SwiftUI’s "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"colorSchemeContrast"},{"type":"text","text":" environment value, UIKit’s "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UIAccessibility.isDarkerSystemColorsEnabled"},{"type":"text","text":" (observed live the same way Reduce Motion is). A multi-stop gradient can dip below an acceptable contrast ratio in places a single flat color never does, so when Increase Contrast is on, every gradient is swapped for a solid "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".label"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".primary"},{"type":"text","text":" tint instead."}]},{"type":"heading","text":"Dynamic Type","level":2,"anchor":"Dynamic-Type"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The default Empty\/Error placeholders’ text scales with Dynamic Type: SwiftUI’s "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ScreenStateDefaultEmptyView"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ErrorView"},{"type":"text","text":" get this for free from "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ContentUnavailableView"},{"type":"text","text":"’s own text styles, and "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ScreenStateDefaultEmptyUIView"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ErrorUIView"},{"type":"text","text":"’s labels use "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UIFont.preferredFont(forTextStyle: .body, compatibleWith:)"},{"type":"text","text":". Both are covered up to the largest accessibility size by snapshot tests, so a regression that silently pins the text to a fixed size would be caught. The default Loading placeholder has no text at all — its ring is a fixed-size shape, unaffected by Dynamic Type by design."}]},{"type":"heading","text":"Identifiers for XCUITest","level":2,"anchor":"Identifiers-for-XCUITest"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Every default placeholder exposes a stable "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"accessibilityIdentifier"},{"type":"text","text":", independent of any localized or custom text, so a UI test can assert which state is showing without string-matching:"}]},{"rows":[[[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Identifier","type":"text"}]}],[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Shown on","type":"text"}]}]],[[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"screenStates.loading"}]}],[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultLoadingView"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultLoadingUIView"}]}]],[[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"screenStates.empty"}]}],[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultEmptyView"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultEmptyUIView"}]}]],[[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"screenStates.error"}]}],[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultErrorView"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultErrorUIView"}]}]],[[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"screenStates.error.retryButton"}]}],[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"The Retry button, when ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"onRetry"},{"text":" is given","type":"text"}]}]],[[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"screenStates.error.secondaryActionButton"}]}],[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"The secondary action button, when ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"secondaryAction"},{"text":" is given","type":"text"}]}]]],"type":"table","header":"row"}]}],"references":{"doc://screenstates.ScreenStates/documentation/ScreenStates/ScreenState/accessibilityAnnouncement":{"role":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenState\/accessibilityAnnouncement","kind":"symbol","type":"topic","fragments":[{"text":"var","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"accessibilityAnnouncement","kind":"identifier"},{"text":": ","kind":"text"},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:SS","text":"String","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":"?","kind":"text"}],"title":"accessibilityAnnouncement","url":"\/documentation\/screenstates\/screenstate\/accessibilityannouncement","abstract":[{"text":"The same text the default placeholders announce to VoiceOver for","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"this case, reusable by a fully custom placeholder that wants to","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"announce transitions with the same wording instead of inventing its","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"own. ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"nil"},{"text":" for ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".data"},{"text":", since at that point a custom placeholder’s","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"own content is what VoiceOver should read, not a transition","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"announcement.","type":"text"}]},"doc://screenstates.ScreenStates/documentation/ScreenStates/ScreenStateDefaultErrorUIView":{"role":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"ScreenStateDefaultErrorUIView"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultErrorUIView","type":"topic","kind":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"class"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"ScreenStateDefaultErrorUIView"}],"title":"ScreenStateDefaultErrorUIView","url":"\/documentation\/screenstates\/screenstatedefaulterroruiview","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Default placeholder shown when a screen’s data failed to load, with an"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"optional Retry button."}]},"doc://screenstates.ScreenStates/documentation/ScreenStates/ScreenStateDefaultErrorView":{"type":"topic","kind":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"ScreenStateDefaultErrorView"}],"title":"ScreenStateDefaultErrorView","abstra